-- title:
--   Both chart orbit relation ideals are generated by their Weierstrass cubics
-- statement:
--   Let $L$ be a period pair, let $D$ be normalized sigma differential data, and let $S_0,\ldots,S_4$ be entire coordinates with no common zero, agreeing off the lattice with
--
--   $$\sigma^3(1,\wp,\wp',\zeta,\wp'\zeta+2\wp^2).$$
--
--   In both affine charts the ideal of all polynomial relations on the available analytic orbit is exactly the principal cubic ideal:
--
--   $$K_0=(y^2-4x^3+g_2x+g_3),$$
--
--   $$K_1=(a-4b^3+g_2a^2b+g_3a^3).$$
--
--   The first chart uses $(t,x,y,u)=(z,S_1/S_0,S_2/S_0,S_3/S_0)$; the second uses $(t,a,b,h)=(z,S_0/S_2,S_1/S_2,S_4/S_2)$. Availability means the displayed denominator is nonzero.
--
--   Consequently, the explicit cubic ideal plus normalized auxiliary polynomial equals the previously defined `extensionFirstCubicChartBaseIdeal`, for every auxiliary polynomial and either chart label. The first-chart equality is reused from its Proved theorem. The new proof establishes the second-chart equality by rational transition, polynomial denominator clearing and exact cancellation of powers of $a$.
--
--   This result proves ideal equality, not merely equality of zero sets or radicals. It does not establish global geometric gluing, select a stabilizer locus, identify cosets with components, or prove the uniform multiplicity degree bound.
-- source:
--   Philippon (1986), Lemmes de zeros dans les groupes algebriques commutatifs, Bull. Soc. Math. France 114, 355-383, section 5, pp. 380-382: ambient defining ideals in the global zero-estimate argument. https://www.numdam.org/item/10.24033/bsmf.2060.pdf. Senthil Kumar K (2026), Appendix A, Theorem A.2. https://doi.org/10.1017/S001309152610145X. Building on the Proved first-chart relation ideal, the new argument identifies the second chart kernel by rational coordinate transition, denominator clearing and exact saturation of its cubic ideal. Both bases are now generated by the explicit cubic and normalized auxiliary polynomial. The budget conditions and all localized lengths are preserved. Full geometric gluing, locus and coset/component selection, and the uniform total-degree estimate remain outside this result.

import Definitions.Def_WeierstrassEllipticZeta_CubicChartBase

open TranscendenceTheory WeierstrassEllipticZeta

theorem WeierstrassEllipticZeta.elliptic_chart_cubic_relation_ideals
    (L : PeriodPair) (D : EllipticSigmaDifferentialData L)
    (S : Fin 5 → ℂ → ℂ)
    (hS : ∀ j, AnalyticOnNhd ℂ (S j) Set.univ)
    (hS_value : ∀ z : ℂ, z ∉ L.lattice → ∀ j : Fin 5,
      S j z = D.sigma z ^ 3 * ![1, L.weierstrassP z, L.derivWeierstrassP z,
        weierstrassZeta L z,
        L.derivWeierstrassP z * weierstrassZeta L z + 2 * L.weierstrassP z ^ 2] j)
    (hS_ne : ∀ z : ℂ, ∃ j : Fin 5, S j z ≠ 0) :
    (∀ c : Fin 2, extensionGlobalChartKernel S c = Ideal.span {extensionChartCubic L.g₂ L.g₃ c}) ∧
      ∀ (Q : MvPolynomial (Fin 7) ℂ) (c : Fin 2),
        extensionCubicChartBaseIdeal L Q c = extensionFirstCubicChartBaseIdeal L S Q c := by sorry
